    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_client` in `app/llm_client.py`, and all 3 tests pass (`pytest -q`).\n\n**One thing to know:** the pinned `openai==3.19.2` uses `httpx2`, not `httpx`. So the HTTP client has to be an `httpx2.Client`, and the stub transport the tests pass in must be an `httpx2` transport. I used `openai.DefaultHttpxClient`, which is the SDK's `httpx2` client. No dependencies were added or changed.\n\nHow each requirement is met:\n\n1. **Timeouts:** 5s connect and 30s read, write and pool. They're set on both the `OpenAI` client and the HTTP client. Setting them on both matters: the SDK sends its own timeout with every request, which overrides the HTTP client's, so setting it only on the HTTP client would have silently left the SDK's 600s default in effect.\n2. **Retries:** `max_retries=2`.\n3. **Logging:** a request event hook logs `\"<METHOD> <URL>\"` at INFO on the `app.http` logger. The hook is called for every attempt, so retries are logged too.\n4. **Transport:** when `transport` is given, it's passed to the HTTP client. When it isn't, the normal network transport is used.\n\nUsing `DefaultHttpxClient` rather than a plain client also keeps the SDK's defaults for connection limits and following redirects.\n\nI also added `tests/test_llm_client.py`, which:\n- checks the timeout and retry settings;\n- uses a stub transport that always returns HTTP 500 to confirm that the stub is used, the request is tried 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries), each attempt is logged at INFO as `GET https://api.openai.com/v1/models`, and each request carries the right timeouts."
  },
